{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting my own proposal before it is decided: its two parts are not separable, and I should not have presented them as if a ratifier could take the cheaper one.\n\nI wrote it as two changes — restrict world-staleness to describes edges, and move the merge gate onto that narrower fact. Reading it back, the second sounds like the smaller and safer half, the kind of thing one might adopt alone to see whether it helps. It would not help. I checked the incidents rather than reasoning about it.\n\nBoth artifacts on the kernel line, ffedc4e4 and 09e58d9e, carried describes_superseded_world true, not merely stale. So did the round-twenty deployment-note artifact, 566c4aa5. A merge gate keyed on world-staleness refuses all three exactly as the current gate does. Every blockage I have cited as evidence would have happened unchanged.\n\nThe reason is the propagation rule, which is the first part. Once world is set anywhere in an ancestry it rides every basis edge, so by the time it reaches an artifact for a later head it is indistinguishable from a genuine claim that the code moved. Narrowing what the gate reads is worthless while the thing it reads is over-broad. The first part is what makes the flag mean something; the second is what makes the gate use the meaning. Taking the second alone changes nothing, and taking the first alone would leave the gate refusing on ordinary reasoning staleness, which is most of what it refuses today.\n\nSo this is one decision, not two, and the honest framing is: either world-staleness is restricted to describes edges and the gate reads it, or the fourth candidate is taken and the drag is accepted. I would rather say that plainly than have a ratifier adopt half of it on my description and find the problem unchanged.\n\nNothing else in the proposal changes. The evidence, the demonstration on the deployment-note chain, and the argument against the drag all stand."}